/-! # Chevalley's scheme-theoretic line stabilizer theorem

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 4.27 and Lemma 4.28, printed pp.94--95,
and Remark 4.30, printed p.96.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-chevalley-scheme-line-stabilizer)

Scalar extension is A tensor E, using the existing Mathlib convention and its canonical
symmetry with E tensor A. The fixed finite basis identifies the general linear group
scheme with automorphisms of these scalar extensions. The equality is on all algebra-valued
points, with the canonical point maps and base-change submodules.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory
open scoped TensorProduct MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Every closed subgroup scheme is the stabilizer of a line in a rational representation.
Source: Milne, Theorem 4.27, Lemma 4.28 and Remark 4.30, printed pp.94--96.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-chevalley-scheme-line-stabilizer)

Proof:
1. Write C = k[G] and H = Spec(C/I) using the closed immersion. The affine finite-type
algebra lemma and the finite-type Noetherian theorem give finite ideal generators of I.
2. The coordinate Hopf algebra and lem-sfb-coalgebra-finite-subcoalgebra give a finite
subcoalgebra V containing 1 and those generators. The rational-representation/comodule
dictionary turns the restricted coproduct into the right regular representation on V.
3. Put W = I intersect V and extend a basis e_1,...,e_d of W to e_1,...,e_n of V.
Write Delta(e_j) = sum_i e_i tensor a_ij. The subgroup laws imply q(a_ij)=0 for
j <= d < i, where q:C to C/I. The counit identity expresses every e_j, j <= d,
as sum_{i>d} epsilon(e_i)a_ij. Thus I is generated by these a_ij.
4. For every k-algebra A, an A-point belongs to H exactly when its evaluations of these
coefficients vanish. This says its action carries W_A into W_A. Apply the same calculation
to its inverse to obtain equality, so H(A) is exactly the stabilizer of W_A.
5. Let E be the d-th exterior power of V and D the span of w=e_1 wedge ... wedge e_d.
The exterior-power free basis gives finite-dimensional E and nonzero w, also when d=0,
where w=1. Base change identifies the ordered wedge bases. Exterior powers of the actions
have matrix entries given by minors; they are natural and multiplicative, so the dictionary
gives a homomorphism of group schemes into GL(E).
6. The exterior basis gives W_A = {v : w wedge v = 0}. An automorphism preserving W_A
preserves its top exterior line D_A. Conversely, preservation of D_A gives wedge^d(alpha)w
= c*w with c a unit; applying wedge^(d+1)(alpha) to w wedge v gives that alpha(v) lies
in W_A. Repeat for alpha inverse. This includes d=0 and d=n.
7. Combine steps 4 and 6. Evaluating the coefficient matrices and their minors commutes
with every algebra homomorphism A to B; the same holds for the base-changed line.
Thus the equality describes the same subfunctor naturally in A. No smoothness, reducedness
or characteristic assumption has been used. -/
theorem exists_rational_line_stabilizer
    {k : Type u} [Field k] (G : Scheme.{u}) [G.Over (Spec (.of k))]
    [GrpObj (G.asOver (Spec (.of k)))] [IsAffine G]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType (G ↘ Spec (.of k))] [QuasiCompact (G ↘ Spec (.of k))]
    (H : Over (Spec (.of k))) [GrpObj H]
    (i : H ⟶ G.asOver (Spec (.of k))) [IsClosedSubgroupScheme i] :
    ∃ (E : Type u) (_ : AddCommGroup E) (_ : Module k E) (_ : FiniteDimensional k E),
      Nontrivial E ∧
      ∃ (ρ : GeneralLinearGroup.RationalHom (G.asOver (Spec (.of k)))
          (RationalRepresentation.FiniteIndex (k := k) E))
        (_ : IsMonHom ρ) (D : Submodule k E),
        Module.finrank k D = 1 ∧
        ∀ (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A],
          Set.range (fun h : points H A ↦ h ≫ i) =
            {g : points (G.asOver (Spec (.of k))) A |
              Submodule.map
                (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.toLin'
                  ((RationalRepresentation.finiteBasis (k := k) E).baseChange A)
                  (GeneralLinearGroup.homMulEquiv
                    (RationalRepresentation.FiniteIndex (k := k) E) k A
                    (g ≫ ρ))).toLinearEquiv.toLinearMap
                (D.baseChange A) = D.baseChange A} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
